  "worked_solution": "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(1),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{\u2032}(1)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(1))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(1).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(1)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(1) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{3}+2x+1 & =1 \\\\ x({x}^{2}+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(1)=0.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{\u2032}(0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{3}+2x+1 \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =3{x}^{2}+2 \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(0) & =3\\cdot 0+2 \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(0) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{\u2032}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
